<title>SPAIN: Spain's &quot;wise men&quot; see 1997 GDP up 2.9 percent.</title>
<headline>Spain's &quot;wise men&quot; see 1997 GDP up 2.9 percent.</headline>
<dateline>MADRID 1997-02-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Spanish GDP will grow 2.9 percent in 1997 and inflation will be 2.7 percent by year-end, the &quot;wise men&quot; advising the Economy Ministry forecast on Tuesday.</p>
<p>At a press conference, the seven economic experts also predicted that Spain would meet the Maastricht convergence targets for inflation and the public deficit this year.  </p>
<p>&quot;Spain can reasonably aspire to meeting the Maastricht budget target this year,&quot; that is, three percent of GDP, according to the group's twice-yearly report.</p>
<p>The average forecast made by the seven economists is for Spain's public deficit to fall to 3.1 percent of GDP in 1997.</p>
<p>GDP growth will be based on the contribution of foreign demand to the economy in 1997, the report said.</p>
<p>252,000 jobs may be created in 1997, bringing the unemployment rate down to 21.5 percent from 22.3 percent in 1996, it added.</p>
<p>-- Madrid newsroom +341 585 2160</p>
